questions.detailed:
A **controlled** component renders its form value from props or state and updates it through an event handler. An **uncontrolled** component lets the browser own the value; React only sets an optional initial value with defaultValue.

## Comparison

| Aspect | Controlled | Uncontrolled |
|---|---|---|
| Source of truth | React state | The DOM node |
| Value prop | value + onChange | defaultValue (optional) |
| Render per keystroke | Yes, for the owning component | No |
| Live validation or masking | Easy | Awkward |
| File inputs | Not possible (read-only value) | The normal approach |
| Reset or programmatic set | Set state | Use a ref or form.reset() |

## Controlled example

```tsx
function NameField() {
  const [name, setName] = useState('');
  const invalid = name.trim().length === 0;
  return (
    <label>
      Name
      <input value={name} onChange={(e) => setName(e.target.value)} aria-invalid={invalid} />
    </label>
  );
}
```

## Uncontrolled example

```tsx
function SearchForm({ onSearch }: { onSearch: (q: string) => void }) {
  return (
    <form
      onSubmit={(e) => {
        e.preventDefault();
        const q = new FormData(e.currentTarget).get('q');
        onSearch(String(q ?? ''));
      }}
    >
      <input name="q" defaultValue="" />
      <button type="submit">Search</button>
    </form>
  );
}
```

## How to choose

- Choose controlled when the UI must react to each change: character counters, dependent dropdowns, input masks, disabling a button until valid.
- Choose uncontrolled for submit-only forms, file uploads, and integrating with non-React widgets.
- Large forms often use libraries (for example react-hook-form) that register inputs as uncontrolled to avoid re-rendering the whole form on each key press. Verify the current library docs for details.

## Common mistakes

- Setting value without onChange, which makes the input read-only and triggers a React warning.
- Switching from undefined to a defined value, which flips an input from uncontrolled to controlled. Initialise state with an empty string.
- Mixing both styles on the same field.
- Storing every field in one state object in a huge form and re-rendering everything per keystroke.

Lifting state up means moving state to the **closest common ancestor** of the components that read or change it. It keeps one source of truth and avoids syncing two copies.

## The drilling problem and the options

| Situation | Preferred option |
|---|---|
| Two siblings share a value | Lift state to their parent |
| Value passes through 2 to 3 layers that do not use it | Composition (children or element props) |
| Many distant consumers, changes rarely | Context |
| Many consumers, frequent updates, derived data | A store or server-state cache |

{{diagram:lift}}

## Composition instead of drilling

Instead of passing user down to an Avatar three layers deep, build the Avatar in the parent and pass it as a slot.

```tsx
type LayoutProps = { header: React.ReactNode; children: React.ReactNode };

function Layout({ header, children }: LayoutProps) {
  return (
    <div>
      <header>{header}</header>
      <main>{children}</main>
    </div>
  );
}

function Page({ user }: { user: User }) {
  // user is used here, so Layout never needs to know about it
  return (
    <Layout header={<Avatar user={user} />}>
      <Feed userId={user.id} />
    </Layout>
  );
}
```

## Practical rules

- Start local. Lift only when a second component needs the value.
- Lifting too high causes wide re-renders, so lift to the nearest common parent, not the app root.
- Composition also helps performance: elements created in a parent keep their identity when the intermediate component re-renders for unrelated reasons.

## Common mistakes

- Putting everything in a global store on day one.
- Reaching for context to avoid two levels of props.
- Lifting state but leaving a duplicate copy in the child, which then drifts.
- Passing a setter several layers down instead of exposing a small callback such as onSelect.

Context lets any descendant read a value without prop drilling. It does **not** offer selective subscription: React compares the provider value with **Object.is**, and if it differs, every consumer of that context re-renders.

## Pitfalls and fixes

| Pitfall | Why it hurts | Fix |
|---|---|---|
| New object in value each render | All consumers re-render whenever the provider does | useMemo the value |
| One big context (user, theme, cart, UI flags) | A cart change re-renders theme readers | Split by domain and change rate |
| State and setters in one value | Components that only dispatch re-render on state change | Separate state and dispatch contexts |
| High-frequency data (mouse, timers) | Constant tree-wide updates | Keep it local, or use a store with selectors |
| Provider placed too high with unstable parent | Parent re-renders cascade | Colocate the provider, pass children through |

{{diagram:ctx}}

## Example

```tsx
const CountState = createContext<number>(0);
const CountDispatch = createContext<React.Dispatch<Action>>(() => {});

function CountProvider({ children }: { children: React.ReactNode }) {
  const [count, dispatch] = useReducer(reducer, 0);
  return (
    <CountState.Provider value={count}>
      <CountDispatch.Provider value={dispatch}>{children}</CountDispatch.Provider>
    </CountState.Provider>
  );
}
```

Dispatch from useReducer is stable, so components that only dispatch never re-render because of count changes. Because the provider receives children as a prop, the children elements keep their identity when the provider's own state changes.

## React version notes

React 19 changes some context syntax (for example rendering the context directly as a provider) and the React Compiler can reduce some manual memoisation. Verify the current documentation before relying on either.

## Common mistakes

- Assuming React.memo blocks context updates. It does not for consumers of the changed context.
- Using context as a general store for frequently updated data.
- Forgetting a sensible default value, which hides a missing provider bug. Throw an error in a custom hook instead.

A custom hook is a function whose name starts with **use** and that calls other hooks. It shares **logic**, not state: each call gets its own state.

## Design checklist

| Concern | Good practice |
|---|---|
| Responsibility | One job, such as useDebouncedValue or useOnlineStatus |
| API | Options object, typed return, stable function identities |
| Side effects | Cleanup, cancellation, and safe under Strict Mode |
| Testability | Test with renderHook from Testing Library |
| Independence | No JSX, no hidden global dependencies |
| Naming | Describes the behaviour, not the component |

## Example

```tsx
function useDebouncedValue<T>(value: T, delayMs = 300): T {
  const [debounced, setDebounced] = useState(value);
  useEffect(() => {
    const id = setTimeout(() => setDebounced(value), delayMs);
    return () => clearTimeout(id);
  }, [value, delayMs]);
  return debounced;
}

function ProductSearch() {
  const [text, setText] = useState('');
  const query = useDebouncedValue(text, 250);
  // fetch using query, not text
  return <input value={text} onChange={(e) => setText(e.target.value)} />;
}
```

## When to extract

- The same effect plus state combination appears in two or more components.
- A component mixes rendering with a subscription lifecycle.
- You want to test logic without rendering UI.

## When not to

- Only to make a long component look shorter, if the grouped state is unrelated.
- When a plain function with no hooks would do. That should not be a hook.
- When it hides a network call that callers would expect to control.

## Common mistakes

- Returning a new object or function every render, which breaks memoised consumers. Memoise where identity matters, and check whether the React Compiler (if adopted) already handles it.
- Calling hooks conditionally inside the hook.
- Creating a hook that wraps a single useState with no added meaning.
- Forgetting that two components using the same hook do not share state. For shared state use context or a store.

Before hooks, class components reused behaviour through HOCs and render props. Hooks (React 16.8) made most of those patterns unnecessary, but you still meet all three in real codebases.

## Comparison

| Aspect | HOC | Render prop | Hook |
|---|---|---|---|
| Shape | withX(Component) | Component calls children(data) | useX() inside component |
| Extra tree nodes | Yes, a wrapper | Yes, a component | None |
| Composition | Wrapper hell with many | Nested callbacks | Plain sequential calls |
| Typing | Hard (injected props) | Moderate | Simple |
| Prop name clashes | Possible | Avoided | Avoided |
| Debugging | Wrapper names in DevTools | Nested function tree | Hooks visible in DevTools |

## Same behaviour three ways

```tsx
// HOC
function withAuth<P extends object>(Wrapped: React.ComponentType<P>) {
  return function Guarded(props: P) {
    const { user } = useAuth();
    return user ? <Wrapped {...props} /> : <Navigate to="/login" />;
  };
}

// Render prop
function Hover({ children }: { children: (hovered: boolean) => React.ReactNode }) {
  const [hovered, setHovered] = useState(false);
  return (
    <div onMouseEnter={() => setHovered(true)} onMouseLeave={() => setHovered(false)}>
      {children(hovered)}
    </div>
  );
}

// Hook
function useHover() {
  const [hovered, setHovered] = useState(false);
  const bind = { onMouseEnter: () => setHovered(true), onMouseLeave: () => setHovered(false) };
  return [hovered, bind] as const;
}
```

## Where each still fits

- **HOC**: wrapping behaviour around a whole component (authorisation, error boundary wrapper, analytics), and libraries that expose them.
- **Render props**: components that own a lifecycle or layout but let the caller control rendering, such as virtualised lists or headless UI components.
- **Hooks**: sharing stateful logic. This is the default.

## Common mistakes

- Creating an HOC inside render, which remounts the component every time.
- Forgetting to hoist statics or forward refs in HOCs.
- Using a render prop where a simple hook is clearer.
- Using a hook for something that needs to wrap the render tree, such as an error boundary.

Without a boundary, an error thrown during rendering unmounts the **entire React tree**, leaving a blank page. An error boundary limits the blast radius.

## What they catch

| Caught | Not caught |
|---|---|
| Errors during rendering of descendants | Event handler errors (use try/catch) |
| Errors in lifecycle methods and constructors | Asynchronous code such as setTimeout or promises |
| Errors in child component trees | Server-side rendering errors |
| | Errors thrown in the boundary itself |

{{diagram:eb}}

## Implementation

Boundaries are still written as **class components** using getDerivedStateFromError and componentDidCatch. Many teams use the react-error-boundary library for a function-friendly API. Verify the current React documentation for any newer options.

```tsx
class Boundary extends React.Component<
  { fallback: React.ReactNode; children: React.ReactNode },
  { failed: boolean }
> {
  state = { failed: false };
  static getDerivedStateFromError() {
    return { failed: true };
  }
  componentDidCatch(error: Error, info: React.ErrorInfo) {
    reportError(error, info.componentStack); // send to monitoring
  }
  render() {
    return this.state.failed ? this.props.fallback : this.props.children;
  }
}

<Boundary fallback={<p>This panel failed to load.</p>}>
  <RecommendationsPanel />
</Boundary>
```

## Placement strategy

- **Top level** as the last resort with a friendly page.
- **Route level** so a broken page does not break navigation.
- **Widget level** around independent, non-critical panels, so the rest stays usable.
- Pair with Suspense so loading and failure states are handled together.

## Common mistakes

- A single boundary at the root only.
- Swallowing errors without logging.
- No reset path, so the fallback is permanent. Offer a retry that resets the boundary key or state.
- Expecting a boundary to catch a rejected fetch promise unless the error is rethrown during render.

Both hooks store state and trigger re-renders. **useReducer** moves the update logic out of event handlers into a pure **reducer** function.

## Comparison

| Aspect | useState | useReducer |
|---|---|---|
| Best for | Independent primitives | Related fields, state machines |
| Updates | setX(value or fn) | dispatch({ type, ... }) |
| Logic location | Scattered in handlers | Centralised in reducer |
| Testing | Through the component | Test the reducer directly |
| Stable updater | setState is stable | dispatch is stable |
| Boilerplate | Minimal | Moderate |

## Example: a request state machine

```tsx
type State =
  | { status: 'idle' }
  | { status: 'loading' }
  | { status: 'success'; data: Item[] }
  | { status: 'error'; message: string };

type Action =
  | { type: 'fetch' }
  | { type: 'resolve'; data: Item[] }
  | { type: 'reject'; message: string };

function reducer(state: State, action: Action): State {
  switch (action.type) {
    case 'fetch':   return { status: 'loading' };
    case 'resolve': return { status: 'success', data: action.data };
    case 'reject':  return { status: 'error', message: action.message };
    default:        return state;
  }
}

const [state, dispatch] = useReducer(reducer, { status: 'idle' });
```

A discriminated union makes impossible states, such as loading with an error message, unrepresentable.

## Rules for reducers

- Keep them **pure**: no fetching, no randomness, no mutation. Strict Mode calls reducers twice in development to expose impurity.
- Return new objects instead of mutating.
- Put side effects in handlers or effects, then dispatch the result.

## When to stay with useState

- A handful of unrelated values.
- A simple toggle or text field.
- No transitions that depend on the current state.

## Common mistakes

- Using useReducer for a single boolean, adding boilerplate.
- Doing async work inside the reducer.
- Stuffing server data into a reducer when a server-state tool would handle caching and refetching.
- Many boolean flags (isLoading, isError, isSuccess) in useState that can contradict each other.

## Timing

{{diagram:timing}}

1. React renders and commits DOM changes.
2. **useLayoutEffect** callbacks run synchronously, before the browser paints.
3. The browser paints.
4. **useEffect** callbacks run, usually after paint.

## Comparison

| Hook | Triggers re-render | Runs | Typical use |
|---|---|---|---|
| useState | Yes, when set | n/a | Data shown in the UI |
| useRef | No | n/a | DOM nodes, timers, latest values, previous values |
| useEffect | No | After paint | Subscriptions, fetching, logging |
| useLayoutEffect | No | Before paint | Measuring and positioning, avoiding flicker |

## Measuring before paint

```tsx
function Tooltip({ anchor, children }: { anchor: React.RefObject<HTMLElement>; children: React.ReactNode }) {
  const tipRef = useRef<HTMLDivElement>(null);
  const [top, setTop] = useState(0);

  useLayoutEffect(() => {
    const a = anchor.current?.getBoundingClientRect();
    const t = tipRef.current?.getBoundingClientRect();
    if (a && t) setTop(a.top - t.height - 8);
  }, [anchor]);

  return <div ref={tipRef} style={{ position: 'fixed', top }}>{children}</div>;
}
```

With useEffect here, the user could see the tooltip at the wrong position for one frame. The state update inside a layout effect triggers a synchronous re-render before paint.

## Refs for values that should not render

```tsx
const latest = useRef(onChange);
useLayoutEffect(() => { latest.current = onChange; });
// Long-lived listeners can call latest.current without re-subscribing.
```

## Notes

- Do not read or write ref.current during rendering, except for lazy initialisation. React documents this as an impurity.
- useLayoutEffect does not run on the server, and server rendering may log a warning. Frameworks offer patterns for this, so verify current guidance.
- Expensive layout effects delay first paint and can hurt INP.

## Common mistakes

- Using useLayoutEffect by default.
- Putting a value in a ref and expecting the UI to update.
- Forgetting cleanup of observers such as ResizeObserver.
- Reading layout properties in a loop, causing layout thrashing.

Function components re-run on every render, and each run creates **new closures** over that render's props and state. A closure is stale when it outlives the render that created it and still reads the old values.

## The classic bug

```tsx
function Counter() {
  const [count, setCount] = useState(0);

  useEffect(() => {
    const id = setInterval(() => {
      setCount(count + 1); // captures count = 0 forever
    }, 1000);
    return () => clearInterval(id);
  }, []); // missing dependency

  return <p>{count}</p>;
}
```

The count stays at 1 because the interval callback always sees the first render's count.

## Fixes

| Fix | When to use |
|---|---|
| Add the dependency | The effect should re-run when the value changes |
| Functional update setCount(c => c + 1) | You only need the previous state |
| Ref holding the latest value | A long-lived listener must read fresh values without re-subscribing |
| Move the logic out of the effect | The effect does not really need the value |
| useReducer | Many dependencies feed one update |

```tsx
useEffect(() => {
  const id = setInterval(() => setCount((c) => c + 1), 1000);
  return () => clearInterval(id);
}, []); // correct: no outside values are read
```

{{diagram:closure}}

## Dependency array rules

- List **every reactive value** the effect reads. The eslint-plugin-react-hooks exhaustive-deps rule helps, so treat its warnings seriously.
- Objects and functions created inside the component change identity every render, so they trigger effects unless memoised or moved inside the effect.
- The React docs describe an Effect Event concept for reading the latest values without re-running effects. It has been experimental, so verify its current status before using it.
- The React Compiler can memoise values automatically in some setups, but it does not remove the need for correct dependencies.

## Common mistakes

- Silencing the lint rule with a comment instead of restructuring.
- Putting an object literal in the array, causing the effect to run on every render.
- Assuming state updates are immediate inside the same closure.
- Using an empty array to mean run once while reading changing values.

Typing quickly in a search box can fire requests A then B. If A returns **after** B, a naive effect sets state with A's results, showing data that does not match the input.

{{diagram:race}}

## Fix with AbortController

```tsx
function Results({ query }: { query: string }) {
  const [items, setItems] = useState<Item[]>([]);
  const [error, setError] = useState<string | null>(null);

  useEffect(() => {
    if (!query) { setItems([]); return; }
    const controller = new AbortController();

    fetch('/api/search?q=' + encodeURIComponent(query), { signal: controller.signal })
      .then((r) => {
        if (!r.ok) throw new Error('Request failed: ' + r.status);
        return r.json();
      })
      .then((data: Item[]) => { setItems(data); setError(null); })
      .catch((e) => {
        if (e.name === 'AbortError') return; // expected on cleanup
        setError(e.message);
      });

    return () => controller.abort();
  }, [query]);

  return error ? <p role="alert">{error}</p> : <List items={items} />;
}
```

When query changes or the component unmounts, cleanup aborts the previous request.

## Options compared

| Technique | Cancels network work | Prevents stale set | Notes |
|---|---|---|---|
| AbortController | Yes | Yes | Preferred when the API supports signals |
| Ignore flag (let ignore = false; cleanup sets true) | No | Yes | Works with any promise |
| Request id comparison | No | Yes | Useful outside React |
| Server-state library | Yes | Yes | Also caching, retries, deduplication |

## Related concerns

- **Debounce** the input to send fewer requests, in addition to cancelling.
- **Strict Mode** in development mounts, unmounts and remounts effects, so a correct cleanup makes the double request harmless.
- Show loading and error states, and keep previous results visible while loading if that suits the UX.
- Frameworks and newer React features (Suspense data fetching, use()) change how this is done. Verify the current documentation for your setup.

## Common mistakes

- Treating AbortError as a real failure and showing an error message.
- Forgetting that an aborted request may still have been processed by the server, so mutations need idempotency.
- Setting state after unmount without any guard.
- Ignoring non-2xx responses, because fetch does not reject on them.

The container and presentational pattern separates **how things work** from **how things look**.

## Roles

| Aspect | Presentational | Container |
|---|---|---|
| Concern | Markup, styling, layout | Data, state, side effects |
| Data source | Props only | APIs, stores, context |
| State | UI state only (open, hover) | Business and server state |
| Reuse | High | Low, tied to a feature |
| Testing | Render with props | Mock data layer |
| Storybook | Easy | Needs mocks |

## Example with a hook as the container

```tsx
// logic
function useOrders(customerId: string) {
  const [orders, setOrders] = useState<Order[]>([]);
  const [loading, setLoading] = useState(true);
  useEffect(() => {
    const c = new AbortController();
    fetch('/api/customers/' + customerId + '/orders', { signal: c.signal })
      .then((r) => r.json())
      .then((d) => { setOrders(d); setLoading(false); })
      .catch(() => {});
    return () => c.abort();
  }, [customerId]);
  return { orders, loading };
}

// view
function OrderList({ orders, loading }: { orders: Order[]; loading: boolean }) {
  if (loading) return <Skeleton rows={5} />;
  return <ul>{orders.map((o) => <li key={o.id}>{o.reference}</li>)}</ul>;
}

// wiring
function OrdersPage({ customerId }: { customerId: string }) {
  const { orders, loading } = useOrders(customerId);
  return <OrderList orders={orders} loading={loading} />;
}
```

## Benefits

- Views can be reused with different data sources.
- Logic can be tested without rendering.
- Designers and developers can iterate on views using fixtures.

## Caveats

- Do not create a container for every tiny component. That is ceremony.
- The strict split matters less once logic lives in hooks. Dan Abramov, who coined the terms, later said he no longer pushes the split as a rule.
- In frameworks with server components, the data-owning part may be a server component. Verify current framework guidance.

## Common mistakes

- Presentational components that secretly call APIs.
- Containers that also contain large amounts of markup.
- Passing dozens of props instead of grouping them into a meaningful object or composing children.

Large forms fail through **performance** (re-rendering everything per keystroke), **complexity** (cross-field rules, steps) and **trust** (client validation alone).

## Architecture

{{diagram:form}}

| Concern | Approach |
|---|---|
| State | One form instance (for example react-hook-form) shared by steps via FormProvider |
| Validation | Schema per step (for example Zod with a resolver); share types with the API |
| Performance | Uncontrolled registration; useWatch or Controller only for fields that must react |
| Steps | Each step validates its own fields via trigger() before moving on |
| Persistence | Save drafts (local or server) so refresh does not lose progress |
| Accessibility | Labels, aria-invalid, error text linked with aria-describedby, focus the first error |
| Server | Revalidate everything; map server errors back to fields with setError |

## Example

```tsx
const schema = z.object({
  email: z.string().email('Enter a valid email'),
  age: z.coerce.number().int().min(18, 'Must be 18 or over'),
});
type FormValues = z.infer<typeof schema>;

function AccountStep() {
  const { register, handleSubmit, formState: { errors } } =
    useForm<FormValues>({ resolver: zodResolver(schema), mode: 'onBlur' });

  return (
    <form onSubmit={handleSubmit((v) => save(v))} noValidate>
      <label>Email <input {...register('email')} aria-invalid={!!errors.email} /></label>
      {errors.email && <p role="alert">{errors.email.message}</p>}
      <label>Age <input type="number" {...register('age')} /></label>
      {errors.age && <p role="alert">{errors.age.message}</p>}
      <button type="submit">Continue</button>
    </form>
  );
}
```

API details differ between library versions, so verify against the current docs.

## Design choices

- **Dynamic fields**: use the library's field array support with stable ids as keys.
- **Dependent fields**: watch only the controlling field.
- **Long forms**: split into steps or sections, and lazy-load heavy step components.
- **Double submit**: disable the button while submitting and use idempotency keys server-side.

## Common mistakes

- Controlled inputs with one giant state object.
- Client-only validation.
- Error messages not announced to assistive technology.
- Losing data when navigating between steps.

Remote data is not like UI state. It is **owned elsewhere**, can change without you knowing, and is usually needed by several components. A server-state library treats it as a cache that must be kept fresh.

## Concepts

| Concept | Meaning |
|---|---|
| Query key | Array identifying the data, such as ['orders', customerId] |
| Query function | Async function that fetches it |
| staleTime | How long data is considered fresh before a background refetch is allowed |
| gcTime (cacheTime in older versions) | How long unused data stays in cache |
| Invalidation | Mark keys stale after a mutation so they refetch |
| Deduplication | Parallel components asking the same key share one request |
| Mutation | Write operation with hooks for optimistic updates and rollback |

```tsx
function Orders({ customerId }: { customerId: string }) {
  const { data, isPending, error } = useQuery({
    queryKey: ['orders', customerId],
    queryFn: ({ signal }) =>
      fetch('/api/customers/' + customerId + '/orders', { signal }).then((r) => r.json() as Promise<Order[]>),
    staleTime: 30_000,
  });

  if (isPending) return <Skeleton />;
  if (error) return <p role="alert">Could not load orders.</p>;
  return <OrderList orders={data} />;
}

function useCancelOrder() {
  const qc = useQueryClient();
  return useMutation({
    mutationFn: (id: string) => fetch('/api/orders/' + id, { method: 'DELETE' }),
    onSuccess: () => qc.invalidateQueries({ queryKey: ['orders'] }),
  });
}
```

Option names and defaults change between major versions, so verify the current documentation.

{{diagram:sq}}

## What it replaces

- Manual loading and error booleans in useState.
- Hand-written race-condition handling and retries.
- Copying fetched data into a global store, where it goes stale.

## When it is not needed

- A single fetch in a small app with no sharing.
- Data supplied by a framework loader or server component that already caches. Check what your framework provides.

## Common mistakes

- Copying query data into local state, creating two sources of truth.
- Query keys that omit variables used in the query function.
- A staleTime of zero everywhere, causing excess refetching.
- Using it for client-only UI state.

A design system is **components plus the process that keeps them consistent**. Without governance it becomes either a bottleneck or an abandoned folder.

{{diagram:ds}}

## Layers

| Layer | Content | Notes |
|---|---|---|
| Tokens | Colour, spacing, type, radius, motion | CSS variables or a token pipeline |
| Primitives | Button, Input, Dialog, Stack | Accessible by default, keyboard support |
| Composed components | DataTable, DatePicker, FormField | Built from primitives |
| Patterns and guidance | Forms, empty states, error handling | Documentation, not code only |

## API design

```tsx
type ButtonProps = React.ButtonHTMLAttributes<HTMLButtonElement> & {
  variant?: 'primary' | 'secondary' | 'danger';
  size?: 'sm' | 'md';
  loading?: boolean;
};

export const Button = React.forwardRef<HTMLButtonElement, ButtonProps>(
  ({ variant = 'primary', size = 'md', loading, children, ...rest }, ref) => (
    <button ref={ref} data-variant={variant} data-size={size} aria-busy={loading} disabled={loading || rest.disabled} {...rest}>
      {children}
    </button>
  )
);
```

Forwarding refs and spreading native props keep components flexible. Newer React versions may treat ref as a normal prop, so verify the current guidance.

## Principles

- **Composition over configuration**: prefer children and slots over dozens of boolean props.
- **Accessibility built in**: focus management, ARIA only where needed, tested with automated and manual checks.
- **Theming**: tokens and CSS variables rather than forking components.
- **Packaging**: ESM output, sideEffects metadata for tree shaking, peer dependency on React.
- **Quality gates**: unit tests, axe checks, visual regression, bundle size checks.
- **Versioning**: semantic versioning, changelogs, codemods and a deprecation window.

## Governance

Define a core team, a contribution path for product teams, a design review step, and adoption metrics. In micro-frontend setups, share the library as a singleton carefully to avoid style and version conflicts.

## Common mistakes

- Building components before tokens.
- Over-flexible components that become unmaintainable.
- Breaking changes without migration paths.
- Treating the library as done instead of a product.
